# Native Layout and Safe Areas

Inspect the existing root layout, route layouts, navigation, and safe-area integration before changing screen layout.

## Safe Areas
- Respect top and bottom system insets.
- Reuse the project's existing safe-area provider and screen wrappers.
- Identify which layer owns each inset.
- Do not apply the same inset twice.
- Do not assume fixed status-bar, home-indicator, or tab-bar heights.
- Do not add safe-area padding blindly to every screen.

## Responsive Layout
- Prefer flex layouts, content-driven dimensions, and appropriate minimum or maximum widths.
- Use useWindowDimensions when layout decisions genuinely depend on available window dimensions.
- Avoid fixed screen heights and arbitrary offsets for primary content.
- Ensure long text can wrap and controls do not become clipped.
- Support narrow phones and larger devices; support landscape and tablets where the product requires them.

## Keyboard
- Inspect existing keyboard and navigation behavior before introducing KeyboardAvoidingView.
- Keep focused inputs and primary actions accessible when the keyboard opens.
- Make long forms scrollable and ensure their final fields remain reachable.
- Avoid stacking multiple keyboard-handling mechanisms without understanding their interaction.

## Navigation Chrome
- Respect headers and tab bars owned by Expo Router layouts.
- Do not duplicate navigation UI inside a route.
- Ensure scrollable content and bottom actions are not obscured.
- Avoid hardcoding tab-bar heights or compensating with arbitrary bottom padding.

## RTL and Accessibility
- Follow the existing RTL strategy.
- Prefer logical start/end alignment where supported and appropriate.
- Check directional icons, back navigation, mixed-language text, and long Arabic labels.
- Provide accessible labels and comfortable touch targets.
- Avoid layouts that break when font scaling increases.

## Verification
Check top and bottom insets, keyboard behavior, narrow layouts, large layouts, text wrapping, and RTL where applicable.

Never mark device-level checks as passed unless they were actually performed.